{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/8/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52364a65\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\n\nRule:\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2ccd9fef\" lines=\"173-187\">\n<description>\nPredict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.\n- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.\n- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.\n\nRule\n- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.\n- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.\n- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c6141b15\" lines=\"84-95\">\n<description>\nSwap a connector segment with repeated marker sprites by connecting the marker anchors and stamping the sprite at the segment endpoints.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors. One foreground color forms a single straight 8-connected segment. The other foreground color forms two or more identical centered sprites.\n\nRule\nBuild a background canvas of the same size. Draw straight connector-color segments between every pair of original sprite anchors. Then stamp the marker sprite, using its centered anchor, onto the two endpoints of the original connector segment.\n\nKey insight\nThe original segment endpoints are the destinations for the repeated sprite, while the original sprite centers become the vertices of the new connector-color complete graph.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7ed72f31\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.\n- Colored sprites appear near red markers.\n\nRule:\n- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.\n- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.\n- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.\n- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.\n\nKey insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b10624e5\" lines=\"24-39\">\n<description>\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Use the top-left quadrant's motif as the source pattern.\n- For each other quadrant, scale the motif by the ratio of that quadrant's red block size to the source red block size.\n- Reflect horizontally for right-side quadrants, vertically for bottom quadrants (both for bottom-right).\n- Align the scaled/reflected motif so its red region overlaps the target anchor, then stamp non-bg pixels.\n\nKey insight: Red block size encodes the scale factor; quadrant position encodes the reflection axes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a834deea\" lines=\"20-39\">\n<description>\nFill each black 5x5 region's missing interior positions with a fixed 3x3 positional color legend.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid uses teal as the dominant background and black for one or more\n  5x5 regions.\n- Each black region has a black one-pixel border and a 3x3 interior that\n  may already contain teal pixels.\n\nRule\n- Overlay the legend 1 7 6 / 4 8 5 / 2 9 3 on the 3x3 interior of every\n  5x5 black region.\n- Wherever an interior non-center legend position is still black, recolor it\n  to the corresponding legend color; leave existing teal pixels, the center,\n  and the black border unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The colored output pixels are not derived from the teal motif shape; they\n  are the fixed position labels for the missing cells of each 3x3 interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\" lines=\"82-97\">\n<description>\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n\nRule\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n\nKey insight\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22208ba4\" lines=\"12-27\">\n<description>\nMove the repeated boundary color's rectangles one block inward while leaving singleton boundary rectangles in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Foreground objects are solid monochrome rectangles touching a grid corner.\n- Exactly one foreground color appears as multiple separate 4-connected objects; other colors appear once.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the multiple objects of the repeated color from their original positions.\n- For each cleared rectangle, translate it away from the row and column border it touches by exactly its height and width.\n- Keep singleton-color objects unchanged; overlapping translated rectangles of the same color merge.\n\nKey insight:\n- Multiplicity selects the moving color, and each boundary rectangle's touched edges determine its inward diagonal translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"abc82100\" lines=\"70-86\">\n<description>\nUse local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.\n- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued\n  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.\n\nRule:\n- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of\n  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.\n- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.\n- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.\n\nKey insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and\nthe anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5792cb4d\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nReverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.\n- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.\n\nRule:\n- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.\n- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.\n- Leave every background pixel unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n<description>\nReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
